  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nWe have entered into a period of much more seasonable temperatures \nand less humid conditions. The rest of this week into the new week \nwill have highs in the 80s and lows in the 60s. This more seasonable \npattern may not last long as the CPC has above normal temperatures \nfor the days 6-10 and 8-14 outlooks. \n\nWildfire smoke from the fires out west in Oregon and Washington is \nriding the ridge. Skies where there aren't clouds will appear hazy \nfrom the smoke. The smoke should remain elevated and not come down \nto the surface. Main effects we will see are vibrant sunrises and \nsunsets where skies are clearer, so get those cameras ready!\n\nThe forecast will be dry in terms of precipitation for the next few \ndays as ridging remains in place off to our west. Moving into \nFriday, the ridging begins to get disturbed as upper wave and \nsurface low pressure moves across the Plains into the Midwest. High \nPWATs return the Illinois as this wave of showers and thunderstorms \nmakes the journey across the region. These showers and thunderstorms \ncould be proficient rain producers. The current QPF for Friday \nthrough Saturday is showing 1-2.5 inches of rainfall, mainly north \nof I-70, with lesser amounts south of I-70. WPC has a slight risk of \nexcessive rainfall for western IL and a marginal risk for eastern \nIL. It does look like the severe risk with this system is low. \nMUCAPE values are only a couple hundred J/kg, but bulk shear values \nare decent. The machine learning models are depicting a lowered to \nno severe threat late this week. Rain chances continue into the new \nweek next week.",
